163
00:11:06,100 --> 00:11:10,778
increased safety concerns around
evaluations themselves. The process of

164
00:11:10,778 --> 00:11:14,419
training models to execute attacks creates
a safety paradox

165
00:11:14,419 --> 00:11:19,139
inherent to control evaluations.
Developers must carefully train models

166
00:11:19,139 --> 00:11:21,679
that can both generate novel attacks and
and hide

167
00:11:21,679 --> 00:11:25,759
their malicious intent, which itself poses
potential risks. As

168
00:11:25,759 --> 00:11:30,600
models approach greater capabilities, this
necessitates additional safeguards for

169
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:35,639
the evaluation process itself. Running
evaluations in sandboxed environments,

170
00:11:35,799 --> 00:11:40,240
limiting model Internet access during
testing, and implementing guardrails

171
00:11:40,240 --> 00:11:44,799
against model exfiltration, developers
must balance thorough testing against

172
00:11:44,799 --> 00:11:48,360
the possibility that the evaluation
process might itself create

173
00:11:48,360 --> 00:11:52,850
dangerous knowledge or capabilities. AI
control games can formalize

174
00:11:52,850 --> 00:11:55,889
the safety usefulness trade off as a
mathematical frontier.

175
00:11:56,529 --> 00:12:00,529
Using game theory, researchers model
control protocols as multi

176
00:12:00,529 --> 00:12:05,490
objective optimization problems that
balance safety against utility. This

177
00:12:05,490 --> 00:12:09,610
approach quantifies the alignment tax how
much usefulness must

178
00:12:09,610 --> 00:12:13,970
be sacrificed to achieve a specific safety
threshold, basically

179
00:12:14,129 --> 00:12:17,370
measuring how much utility usefulness you
lose if you

180
00:12:17,370 --> 00:12:20,409
insist that the model is safe. The
resulting safety

181
00:12:20,409 --> 00:12:24,129
usefulness frontier represents the set of
achievable trade offs

182
00:12:24,129 --> 00:12:29,330
between preventing unacceptable outcomes
and preserving system utility. This

183
00:12:29,330 --> 00:12:32,129
type of formal analysis can tell us the
thresholds

184
00:12:32,129 --> 00:12:37,049
after which marginal safety improvements
require disproportionate utility
sacrifices.

185
00:12:37,850 --> 00:12:42,090
For example, increasing human audit rates
from 0.1% to

186
00:12:42,090 --> 00:12:47,059
1% may minimally improve safety while
significantly increasing costs

187
00:12:47,059 --> 00:12:50,940
and decreasing system throughput. Then we
can make educated

188
00:12:50,940 --> 00:12:53,899
decisions about what level of utility to
safety we

189
00:12:53,899 --> 00:12:56,740
are willing to trade versus not. There is
figure

190
00:12:56,740 --> 00:13:01,259
5.53 in the textbook. Here it depicts
illustration of

191
00:13:01,259 --> 00:13:04,299
how applying a safety or alignment
technique could make

192
00:13:04,299 --> 00:13:07,940
the model less capable. This is called
