193
00:13:08,340 --> 00:13:12,100
Audits serve as the fundamental
verification mechanism in control

194
00:13:12,259 --> 00:13:16,710
protocols. In control evaluations, an
audit refers to the

195
00:13:16,710 --> 00:13:20,509
process where human experts review model
outputs or actions

196
00:13:20,509 --> 00:13:24,549
to determine if they constitute attack
attempts. Unlike automated

197
00:13:24,549 --> 00:13:27,350
monitoring, which can be applied broadly
but may miss

198
00:13:27,350 --> 00:13:31,909
subtle attacks, human audits provide high
confidence verification but

199
00:13:31,909 --> 00:13:33,909
can only be applied to a small fraction of

200
00:13:33,909 --> 00:13:38,549
model actions due to resource constraints.
Effective auditing includes

201
00:13:38,549 --> 00:13:42,740
several components randomly selecting
actions for review to prevent

202
00:13:42,740 --> 00:13:46,659
models from predicting audit timing,
applying extra scrutiny to

203
00:13:46,659 --> 00:13:51,220
actions flagged by automated monitors, and
maintaining consistent evaluation

204
00:13:51,299 --> 00:13:56,220
criteria across auditors. The audit budget
what percentage of

205
00:13:56,220 --> 00:14:00,019
actions can realistically receive human
review represents one of

206
00:14:00,019 --> 00:14:04,460
the most important constraints when
designing control protocols. Analysis

207
00:14:04,460 --> 00:14:07,759
of control evaluations on toy models give
us information

208
00:14:07,840 --> 00:14:12,519
on designing effective control protocols.
Control evaluations have yet

209
00:14:12,519 --> 00:14:16,000
to see proper extensive usage in frontier
AI labs.

210
00:14:16,399 --> 00:14:20,120
Despite this, some analysis using toy
models has revealed

211
00:14:20,120 --> 00:14:25,080
several important principles for
preventing concentrated catastrophes. The
timing

212
00:14:25,080 --> 00:14:28,600
of auditing matters a lot if AI systems
can

213
00:14:28,600 --> 00:14:31,519
determine when they're being monitored,
even a high audit

214
00:14:31,519 --> 00:14:34,779
rate might be insufficient. However, if
the timing of

215
00:14:34,779 --> 00:14:37,860
audits is truly random, much lower audit
rates can

216
00:14:37,860 --> 00:14:42,299
still provide strong safety guarantees.
The difficulty of coordination

217
00:14:42,299 --> 00:14:46,419
between AI instances can be leveraged for
safety if

218
00:14:46,419 --> 00:14:49,899
multiple AI systems must coordinate to
cause harm. Making

219
00:14:49,899 --> 00:14:53,100
coordination harder, for instance, by
increasing the number of

220
00:14:53,100 --> 00:14:56,379
instances that must coordinate, or the
difficulty of finding

221
00:14:56,379 --> 00:15:01,200
coordination signals by using anti
steganography measures can dramatically

222
00:15:01,200 --> 00:15:05,279
improve safety. Even with relatively low
audit rates, not

223
00:15:05,279 --> 00:15:08,879
all episodes are equally risky. By
identifying which types

224
00:15:08,879 --> 00:15:12,519
of inputs or situations present higher
risks and focusing

225
00:15:12,519 --> 00:15:16,480
auditing resources there, protocols can
achieve better safety with

226
00:15:16,480 --> 00:15:19,799
the same resources. This remains true even
if the

227
00:15:19,799 --> 00:15:23,000
AI systems know which episodes we think
